# THE ONE RULE IN THIS FILE, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE USUAL ONE.
#
# `src/services/` holds GENERATED code, and it is COMMITTED. That is the whole
# point of this repository: `cafaye-ts` ships a client built from the fleet's
# OpenAPI documents, and the build a self-hoster runs is a `npm ci` that
# touches no network but npm. Generation happens on a maintainer's machine, on
# purpose, and its output is reviewed in a diff like any other change.
#
# So do NOT add `src/services/`, `dist/`, or any `*.gen.ts` to this file. A
# gitignore entry for the generated tree turns "generated from the spec, and
# committed" into "generated at install time", silently, and the only symptom is
# that `git diff --exit-code` in test/regeneration.test.mjs starts passing
# vacuously over a tree it can no longer see. Two tests exist to catch exactly
# that mistake: `regeneration.test.mjs` proves a hand-edit is reverted, and
# `generated-is-tracked.test.mjs` asserts the committed files are really tracked
# by git. A gitignore entry would fail the second one.

# Dependencies. `npm ci` from the committed package-lock.json.
node_modules/

# Build output. The published entry points are `src/index.ts` and the generated
# tree; `dist/` is only ever produced by an explicit `npm run build`.
dist/
*.tgz

# Hey-api's own crash-report and log artefacts. It writes these next to the
# project root when something fails mid-generation, and they are the first thing
# that should be untracked: a crash log committed alongside the tree it failed
# to generate is a confusing thing to find in a diff.
*.log
openapi-ts-crash-*.json
hey-api-report-*.json
.logs/

# Editor and OS noise — the same set the sibling repositories carry.
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# A scratch file that test/readme-examples.test.mjs writes at the repository
# root so the README's TypeScript can be compiled against this package's own
# `exports` map. The test deletes it in a `finally`; this line is the safety net
# for the case where it does not get to, and it is the only entry in this file
# that ignores a TypeScript file — which is worth stating plainly, given the rule
# at the top of this one.
readme-examples.probe.ts

# Local environment. There are no secrets in this repository: the client holds
# no credentials and reads none from disk. These two lines are here because a
# `.env` that appears in a self-hoster's checkout is their file, not ours.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
